{"name":"Roman Wall","hex":"#b0ada0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"roman","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Upright, as opposed to italic.","Of or related to the Latin alphabet or roman numerals."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the main three types used for the Latin alphabet (the others being italics and blackletter), in which the ascenders are mostly straight.","Ellipsis of roman numeral."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/roman"},{"term":"wall","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rampart of earth, stones etc. built up for defensive purposes.","A structure built for defense surrounding a city, castle etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To enclose with, or as if with, a wall or walls.","To use a wallhack."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wall"}],"entries":[{"title":"Roman Wall (Sofia)","blocks":[{"text":"The Roman Wall is the popular but incorrect name for an early Ottoman-era architectural monument in Sofia, the capital of Bulgaria. Part of a 16th or 17th-century Islamic religious building, it now stands in the middle of a small marketplace in the Lozenets neighbourhood."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Sofia-Rimskata-stena-3.jpg/330px-Sofia-Rimskata-stena-3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Wall_(Sofia)","wikidata":"Q30066899"},{"title":"Ancient Roman defensive walls","blocks":[{"text":"Defensive walls are a feature of ancient Roman architecture. The Romans generally fortified cities, rather than building stand-alone fortresses, but there are some fortified camps, such as the Saxon Shore forts like Porchester Castle in England. City walls were already significant in Etruscan architecture, and in the struggle for control of Italy under the early Republic many more were built, using different techniques. These included tightly fitting massive irregular polygonal blocks, shaped to fit exactly in a way reminiscent of later Inca work. The Romans called a simple rampart wall an agger; at this date great height was not necessary. The Servian Wall around Rome was an ambitious project of the early 4th century BC. The wall was up to 10 metres (32.8 ft) in height in places, 3.6 metres (12 ft) wide at its base, 11 km (7 mi) long, and is believed to have had 16 main gates, though many of these are mentioned only from writings, with no other known remains. Some of it had a fossa or ditch in front, and an agger behind, and it was enough to deter Hannibal. Later the Aurelian Wall replaced it, enclosing an expanded city, and using more sophisticated designs, with small forts at intervals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Servian_Wall-Termini_Station.jpg/330px-Servian_Wall-Termini_Station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Roman_defensive_walls","wikidata":"Q7362352"},{"title":"Wall","blocks":[{"text":"A wall is a structure and a surface that defines an area; carries a load; provides security, shelter, or soundproofing; or serves a decorative purpose. There are various types of walls, including border barriers between countries, brick walls, defensive walls in fortifications, and retaining walls that hold back dirt, stone, water, or noise. Walls can also be found in buildings, where they support roofs, floors, and ceilings, enclose spaces, and provide shelter and security."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Noel_2005_P%C3%A9kin_031_muraille_de_chine_Mutianyu.jpg/330px-Noel_2005_P%C3%A9kin_031_muraille_de_chine_Mutianyu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall","wikidata":"Q42948"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Muralla_de_Lugo_-_03.jpg/500px-Muralla_de_Lugo_-_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Muralla_de_Lugo_-_03.jpg/1280px-Muralla_de_Lugo_-_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Muralla_de_Lugo_-_03.jpg/500px-Muralla_de_Lugo_-_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Section of the Roman walls of Lugo, Spain, 263–276 AD","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuralla_de_Lugo_-_03.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Trier_Porta_Nigra_BW_2.JPG/500px-Trier_Porta_Nigra_BW_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Trier_Porta_Nigra_BW_2.JPG/1280px-Trier_Porta_Nigra_BW_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Trier_Porta_Nigra_BW_2.JPG/500px-Trier_Porta_Nigra_BW_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Porta Nigra of Trier, Germany, capital of the Roman province of Gallia Belgica, constructed between 186 and 200 AD.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrier_Porta_Nigra_BW_2.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Hisarya_Fortress_057.jpg/500px-Hisarya_Fortress_057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Hisarya_Fortress_057.jpg/1280px-Hisarya_Fortress_057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Hisarya_Fortress_057.jpg/500px-Hisarya_Fortress_057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diocletianopolis city walls